# QAEverest

The `qaeverest` MCP server is a QA copilot: it generates test cases (from a story, a spec, or this repo's own code), builds and runs automation suites, tests AI agents, and runs security and performance checks against a running target. Reach for it when the user asks for **test cases**, a **test plan**, **API tests**, **UI or mobile automation scenarios**, **running a suite**, a **security scan**, or a **load/performance test** — not for ordinary unit tests you can write directly in the repo.

## Picking the tool

| The user is asking for | Tool |
|---|---|
| Functional / system / integration (SIT) / database test cases from a story or requirement | `qae_generate_testcases` |
| API tests, or tests from an OpenAPI/Swagger spec or Postman collection | `qae_generate_api_tests` |
| End-to-end browser flows (navigate / click / type / assert) | `qae_generate_ui_tests` |
| Mobile app flows (gestures, device states, platform behaviour) | `qae_generate_mobile_tests` |
| Tests for **this repo's code** rather than a story | `qae_build_inventory`, then `qae_generate_from_features` |
| Something vague, or that mixes generating with building or running a suite | `qae_chat` |
| Running an existing automation suite against an environment | `qae_list_suites`, then `qae_run_suite` |
| Running a saved AI agent suite and its release gate | `qae_agent_suites`, then `qae_agent_run` |
| Security headers, SSL/TLS, common vulnerabilities on a URL | `qae_security_scan` |
| Load, stress, spike or soak metrics for a URL | `qae_performance_test` |
| Credits left, requests this month, which services are enabled | `qae_status` |

`qae_generate_ui_tests` and `qae_generate_mobile_tests` run as background jobs and can take a few minutes. The `qae_generate_*` tools produce test cases — none of them drives a live browser or device; `qae_run_suite` is what executes.

## Working with the results

- Pass `domain_name` and `module_name` when the repo makes them obvious; put focus areas, constraints and known edge cases in `additional`. Vague input produces vague coverage.
- Generated scenarios come back as Markdown. Save them under `qaeverest-tests/` as `.feature` files unless the repo already has a test layout — in which case follow it.
- Offer to scaffold automation (Playwright, Cypress, RestAssured, …) from the scenarios once the user has picked the ones they want. Generate the test cases first; don't invent scenarios of your own and present them as QAEverest output.
- `qae_chat` may answer `needs_input` (ask the user those questions, then call again with `answers`) or `awaiting_approval` (ask the user, then `qae_approve` with THEIR decision — never approve on their behalf).

## Before you call

- **Every generation, scan, suite run and performance run costs QAEverest credits.** Don't call a tool speculatively, and don't retry a failed call in a loop. If the user is exploring, run `qae_status` first.
- **Only scan, load-test or run suites against hosts the user is authorized to test.** Ask before pointing `qae_security_scan`, `qae_performance_test` or `qae_run_suite` at anything that isn't obviously the user's own environment; `qae_performance_test` sends real traffic at the target.
- If a tool reports its service is disabled, say so plainly — an admin enables it under **Admin → API Management**; retrying won't help.
